<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Imamate of Aussa</h1><p class="st-meta">1577 CE – 1672 CE (approx.) · capital Aussa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last refuge of the old Adal state, moved from Harar to the Aussa oasis in the Afar lowlands to escape Oromo attacks.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</a> and decades of war, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> was weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>. In 1577 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Muhammad Jasa moved the seat of government to the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awash-river">Awash</a> river, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> lowlands, creating a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> claimed to continue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal</a>'s religious leadership but ruled a much smaller state, dependent on the irrigated farmland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a> oasis and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> pastoralists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> was left under its own governors. Raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and Afar groups and disputes over succession kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> weak.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> disintegrated in the later 17th century, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> reduced to figureheads and finally overthrown, traditionally around 1672. In 1734 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudaito-dynasty">Mudaito</a> clan founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aussa-sultanate">Aussa Sultanate</a> on its former lands, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> became the separate <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/harar-emirate">Harar Emirate</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imamate-of-aussa.jpg" alt="Imamate of Aussa" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: burts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1577 CE – 1672 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aussa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad Jasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Harari, Afar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aussa Imamate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=imamate-of-aussa" aria-label="Imamate of Aussa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5767 1885 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imamate of Aussa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6135 2044h0M6140 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6087 2141h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6087 2141h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ethiopia. <a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=imamate-of-aussa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
